-- title:
--   Kernel degree p^{hm} of [p^m] in height h
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $B$ be a Noetherian commutative ring, and let $X$ be a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $B$ in the sense of the structure `FormalODModule`: a two-variable formal group law $F$ over $B$ together with the hypothesis that $F$ is commutative, an action $a \mapsto X.\mathrm{act}\,a$ of $\mathbb Z_{p^2} = W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ by pairs of power series in two variables which are law endomorphisms of $F$ and satisfy $\mathrm{act}\,1 = \mathrm{id}$, $\mathrm{act}(ab) = \mathrm{act}\,a \circ \mathrm{act}\,b$ and $\mathrm{act}(a+b) = \mathrm{act}\,a +_F \mathrm{act}\,b$, and a further law endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = \mathrm{act}\,p$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}\,a = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ for the Witt-vector Frobenius $\sigma$. Assume $X$ has height $h$, that is, the series $\mathrm{act}\,p$ has kernel of degree $p^{h}$. Then for every natural number $m$ the series $\mathrm{act}(p^{m})$ has kernel of degree $p^{hm}$; here a pair of series $\varphi$ has kernel of degree $d$ when the quotient of $B[[x_1,x_2]]$ by the ideal generated by the components of $\varphi$ is finite and projective as a $B$-module and, for every field $\kappa$ and every ring homomorphism $f : B \to \kappa$, the corresponding quotient formed from the coefficientwise image of $\varphi$ has $\kappa$-dimension exactly $d$.
--
--   This is the multiplicativity of kernel degrees along the powers of the uniformiser $p$ on a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module: height $h$ for $[p]$ propagates to degree $p^{hm}$ for $[p^m]$. It serves as the exponent bookkeeping used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld dictionary for special formal modules, and is invoked by the results computing kernel degrees of endomorphisms that differ from a central power of $p$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_HasHeight_hasKernelOfDegree_act_pow.lean

